From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 19:23:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: Back the full units' parameter buffer
 on demand

Every rendering VM backs two full render units with a 256 MiB PB heap
each at creation, about 512 of the ~930 MiB a rendering client costs,
while the macOS driver starts its parameter buffer at about 8 MiB and
grows it with use. The firmware only ever receives the page and block
records of a manager's seeded blocks, counted from the heap's start, so
the blocks above a manager's seed need no backing while it is live.

Add g16_pb_lazy (default 0, latched at its first read). With N set, a
full unit keeps its whole heap window and address but backs only its
first N blocks (1 means 256 blocks, 32 MiB). The in-flight free-block
ring samples that g16_pb_size takes are also kept per VM; in SUBMIT,
under the VM's resource lock, each unit's backing grows to three times
the widest distance its VM was seen at, or to the whole window once a
render held three quarters of its manager. Growth only appends, in at
most eight steps, and raises the backed count after the new blocks are
mapped and published. Managers are seeded at publication within the
backed count, so no manager is handed an unmapped block, and InitBM
takes the manager's own count. Backing is never released before the
unit itself.

The M4 firmware has no growth protocol: a render that outgrows its
manager still faults, as with g16_pb_blocks. Growth follows the
samples, so it protects the next command, not the one in flight. The
stats file gains a pb_lazy line.

Assisted-by: LLM
